In Linux and Unix systems, a dashed filename refers to a file or directory whose name starts with a dash (-)
or double dash (--).Because command-line utilities use the dash (-) character to flag options and arguments 
(e.g., rm -r), a file named -filename or simply - will break standard commands. 
The shell misinterprets the filename as a command option, leading to errors or unexpected behavior

Using plain cat -file will fail because cat tries to read an option named -f

solution -> Explicit Path: Use a relative path prefix like ./ so the command sees the path instead of a leading dash.
